,{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introduction","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"JS9dMkOzQD"}],"identifier":"introduction","label":"Introduction","html_id":"introduction","implicit":true,"key":"uaEoQzXfWU"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this section we will be following along with and completing course material from the Pentester Academy’s SecurityTube Linux Assembly language Expert 64 Bit course. The third assignment is attempting to reduce the instruction size and remove nulls from the Hello World program written in the previous section. We will show some techniques that can be used to accomplish making the code more compact and to remove nulls within the opcodes.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"qibjNCEkFA"}],"key":"W9G1ylk6Oh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compact code without nulls is desireable for shellcode, since often the memory buffers that are available within vulnerabilities are limited. In this section, we will code, assemble, link and run the enhanced version of helloworld.nasm, while making references to the original version. We will run the enhanced version of the shellcode to verify that it still works as before. Execution is to be monitored via GDB. We will use the GEF or PEDA add-ons for GDB to enhance the visual presentation from GDB.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"yytFwOXxm5"}],"key":"eaxj5HctFs"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Project code is contained in my ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"lin